 # H10 fixture · browser activation trap
 
-This renamed Web plugin is valid on the host side but its browser bundle still
-registers a legacy identity. Exam material only — do not publish (`"private": true`).
+This renamed Web plugin is valid on the host side and its browser bundle
+registers under the package name the host composes the boot row from. Exam
+material only — do not publish (`"private": true`).
+
+## Extension points
+
+- **Composition** — `dsh.bundle.patch` points at `cordis.patch.yml`, whose single
+  `insert` row mounts this package (`id: bench-browser-activation`,
+  `name: "@demo/dsh-bench-browser-activation"`). Listing the package name in a
+  profile's `dsh.profile.bundles` is enough to mount it; no profile-local patch
+  is needed.
+- **Browser half** — `dsh.client` (`platform: "web"`) plus the `./client`
+  export makes `client.js` this package's `/plugins` bundle in
+  `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-modules`.
+- **Registration** — `client.js` is the loader's lazy-CJS factory artifact: a
+  classic script (no ESM syntax) that calls
+  `window.__ModuleLoader__.load({ id, factory })`. `id` must be the resolved
+  package name, because the kernel keys the boot row by that name and refuses a
+  bundle that registers any other id. Renaming the package therefore requires
+  updating `client.js` in the same change.
